## Where each copyright line came from

Every line above was read from the upstream's own files rather than inferred. This section records which, so the claims can be re-checked instead of taken on trust.

**rs-matter 0.1.0 and libertas-matter 0.1.1.** The copyright line is carried by the upstream source files themselves, `src/codec/base38.rs` and `src/utils/select.rs`. Both crates declare `license = "Apache-2.0"` and ship no separate licence file, so `LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt` holds the canonical Apache License 2.0 text.

**storekey 0.6.0.** All three files come from this one version: `src/decode.rs`, `src/decode/read.rs` and `src/encode.rs` respectively. The crate ships a verbatim Apache License 2.0 text with the copyright appendix left unfilled, and none of its source files carries a copyright header, so the holders are taken from the crate's own `authors` field.

An automated scan attributed two of these files differently - `encode.rs` to `bytekey-fix` 0.5.0 `src/ser.rs` and `decode/read.rs` to storekey 0.5.0. Both were the scanner following an ancestor rather than our actual source: storekey is itself a fork of bytekey, and storekey 0.5.0 and 0.6.0 differ only slightly here. Our files match 0.6.0 - decisively for `read.rs`, which carries 0.6.0's elided `impl Read for SliceReader<'_>` rather than 0.5.0's `impl<'a> ... <'a>`. Dan Burkert's copyright from the bytekey lineage is carried by storekey's own authors list.

**oxigraph 0.4.0-alpha.7 and 0.5.0-beta.2.** The published `.crate` archives ship no licence file. The copyright line is taken from `LICENSE-MIT` in the upstream repository.

**nourl 0.1.4.** Neither the published `.crate` nor the upstream repository contains any licence file; the crate declares only `license = "MIT / Apache-2.0"`. The holder is taken from the crate's `authors` field, and `LICENSES/MIT-nourl.txt` reproduces the canonical MIT text with that copyright line.

**http 1.x.** The copyright line is taken verbatim from the crate's own `LICENSE-MIT`.

